// Package-level bump allocator over one qualified `Extent`
// (wiki/spec/resources/allocation.md). The compiler owns no allocator
// semantics here: it only checks that the package conserves the `Granted`
// custody it was handed, through `Granted::content`, `old`, and `separate`.
//
// Every extent the allocator touches is `Extent in Granted & Vacant`:
// granted authority over a range carrying no live resident. `Vacant` is the
// dormant-content state of the placed-access contract
// (wiki/spec/resources/placed_access.md), and ordinary extent split/merge
// reject resident content, so `merge` and `reset` demanding `Vacant` parts
// are what enforce cleanup-before-return: an issued extent still holding a
// `Resident<P, T>` does not satisfy the returned-region parameters, and a
// container must retire its residents to `Vacant` before release or reset.
// Establishing the resident still waits on the undeclared place/take/read
// operations; this pins the dormant-custody shape those operations must
// satisfy.
//
// Beside the tail extent the strategy carries `remaining`, a counted byte
// residual — the contract keeps it a distinct premise from the tail's
// placement authority (wiki/spec/resources/allocation.md: "A counted byte
// residual summarizes capacity; the tail extent supplies placement. These
// are distinct premises."). The infallible request bounds the caller's
// demand on that count: `allocate` requires `length <= strategy.remaining`,
// the exact capacity requirement.
//
// Carrying the count is necessary, not cosmetic, and the caller must
// restate it per request:
// - `split`'s conservation law fixes only the union of the two parts'
//   content and never `result.taken.length`, so the residual left after an
//   allocation is not derivable through `separate` and cannot be discharged
//   against `tail.length`.
// - The decrement cannot be published either: a machine whose signature
//   carries `Granted` content has its `ensures` validated as a
//   content-conservation contract, admitting only qualification clauses,
//   exact content projections, and `separate` compositions — a scalar count
//   equality is outside that grammar.
// - And the next residual cannot be computed at the call site: the requires
//   check does not propagate a subtraction's lower bound (`capacity - 16`
//   cannot prove `>= 32`), and a param's `requires` bounds do not survive
//   the first request's consumption of the backing — so `exercise` grants
//   the second request a stated residual floor equal to its own demand
//   (`remaining: 32`), the minimal authority the call needs.
// `reset` restores the residual from the recomposed range's own geometry.
//
// Shape decisions a next reader needs:
//
// - `Granted` cannot be minted from geometry, and a checked callable may
//   retain content-bearing custody only from a consumed owned input. The
//   split therefore consumes the whole tail; a `&mut` carve was rejected
//   because its result custody would come from a borrowed parameter.
//   Partitioning is a boundary requirement: the package authors the exact
//   `separate` law, and the selected provider is what establishes the two
//   qualified sub-ranges. No ordinary machine here constructs `Extent`
//   geometry.
// - Recomposition takes one `Split` record rather than two `Extent`
//   parameters: a boundary returning `Granted` custody from two consumed
//   qualified inputs is rejected as ambiguous, and the checker's exact
//   correspondence recognizes only a one-parameter identity, not `separate`.
//   An ordinary machine faces no such limit: `reset` consumes `lower` and
//   `upper` as two separate qualified parameters.
// - A record returned by a call does not surface its fields' declared
//   `in Granted` facts on the result's field places, so `issued.allocation`
//   cannot fill an `in Granted` parameter directly. Each qualified field is
//   restated through an exactly typed `let`
//   (`let field: Extent in Granted & Vacant = issued.allocation`), which
//   re-reads the declared field domain; the same holds for `Split` fields
//   returned by the boundary. The restated fields of one record then share
//   their custody: once a restated sibling value is consumed onward — by a
//   call or by a plain `let` transfer — the record's other restated facts
//   are gone. `exercise` therefore binds `first_allocation` only after
//   `first_tail` has been consumed, and consumes `second_allocation`
//   together with `second_tail` in the `reset` call. A container chaining
//   allocation must order custody this way or keep the family in one
//   record consumed atomically.
// - An ordinary machine cannot restate the boundary's `separate` law as
//   its own `ensures`, so `allocate` and `reset` keep the exact
//   conservation inside their bodies instead of publishing it.
// - Returning an allocation does not rewind the tail. A returned region
//   stays a separate qualified value until reset, which consumes every
//   returned region together with the tail and recomposes the original
//   range. Because `Extent` is linear, a still-live allocation cannot be
//   dropped to reach reset; a partial reset has no spelling.
use omega::language::core::extent;
use omega::language::core::content;

// Exact partition of one qualified vacant range into two adjacent qualified
// vacant ranges.
data Split {
    taken: Extent in Granted & Vacant;
    rest: Extent in Granted & Vacant;
}

boundary trait ExtentPartition {
    machine split(whole: Extent in Granted & Vacant, length: u64) -> Split
    ensures
        Granted::content(old(&whole))
        == separate(
            Granted::content(&result.taken),
            Granted::content(&result.rest),
        );

    machine merge(parts: Split) -> Extent
    ensures
        result in Granted & Vacant,
        separate(
            Granted::content(old(&parts.taken)),
            Granted::content(old(&parts.rest)),
        )
        == Granted::content(&result);
}

// Bump policy: the allocation is the low end of the tail; the rest is the new
// tail. Nothing about the allocation's later cleanup moves the tail.
// `remaining` is the counted byte residual summarizing the tail's capacity;
// it is a separate premise from the tail's placement authority.
data Bump {
    tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant;
    remaining: u64;
}

data Issued {
    strategy: Bump;
    allocation: Extent in Granted & Vacant;
}

// Infallible request: the caller proves the exact capacity requirement
// `length <= strategy.remaining` (allocation.md), and the body decrements
// the residual by the same length the requires bounds — the requires
// ordering is what licenses the subtraction.
machine allocate(partition: &ExtentPartition, strategy: Bump, length: u64) -> Issued
requires
    length <= strategy.remaining
reaches ExtentPartition
{
    let tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = strategy.tail;
    let parts: Split = partition.split(tail, length);
    let taken: Extent in Granted & Vacant = parts.taken;
    let rest: Extent in Granted & Vacant = parts.rest;
    Issued {
        strategy: Bump {
            tail: rest,
            remaining: strategy.remaining - length,
        },
        allocation: taken,
    }
}

// Reset after full return. `lower` was issued before `upper`, so the original
// range is `lower ++ upper ++ tail`. Both returned regions are consumed, and
// both must still be vacant: a region whose resident has not been retired
// does not type as `Vacant` and cannot reach recomposition.
machine reset(
    partition: &ExtentPartition,
    strategy: Bump,
    lower: Extent in Granted & Vacant,
    upper: Extent in Granted & Vacant
) -> Bump
reaches ExtentPartition
{
    let tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = strategy.tail;
    let upper_and_tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = partition.merge(Split {
        taken: upper,
        rest: tail,
    });
    let whole: Extent in Granted & Vacant = partition.merge(Split {
        taken: lower,
        rest: upper_and_tail,
    });
    let restored_residual: u64 = whole.length;
    Bump { tail: whole, remaining: restored_residual }
}

// Two coexisting allocations, cleanup of each, then reset to the whole range,
// chained through the package machines `allocate` and `reset`. The caller
// proves the exact capacity requirement at the entry — `capacity` covers the
// whole 16 + 32 plan and equals the backing's geometry — then grants each
// request its stated residual: the full `capacity` first, then the literal
// floor `32`, since the derived residual `capacity - 16` cannot be spelled
// or proved here. Each returned record's qualified fields are restated
// through exactly typed locals in the custody order described in the header.
machine exercise(
    partition: &ExtentPartition,
    whole: Extent in Granted & Vacant,
    capacity: u64
) -> Extent
requires
    48 <= capacity;
    capacity == whole.length
reaches ExtentPartition
{
    let issued_first: Issued = allocate(
        partition,
        Bump { tail: whole, remaining: capacity },
        16,
    );
    let first_tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = issued_first.strategy.tail;
    let issued_second: Issued = allocate(
        partition,
        Bump { tail: first_tail, remaining: 32 },
        32,
    );
    let second_residual: u64 = issued_second.strategy.remaining;
    let second_tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = issued_second.strategy.tail;
    let second_allocation: Extent in Granted & Vacant = issued_second.allocation;
    let first_allocation: Extent in Granted & Vacant = issued_first.allocation;
    let restored: Bump = reset(
        partition,
        Bump { tail: second_tail, remaining: second_residual },
        first_allocation,
        second_allocation,
    );
    restored.tail as Extent
}

// The same composition spelled directly over the partition boundary, kept as
// the control surface for restating `Split` fields from a boundary result.
machine exercise_boundary(
    partition: &ExtentPartition,
    whole: Extent in Granted & Vacant
) -> Extent
reaches ExtentPartition
{
    let first: Split = partition.split(whole, 16);
    let first_allocation: Extent in Granted & Vacant = first.taken;
    let after_first: Extent in Granted & Vacant = first.rest;
    let second: Split = partition.split(after_first, 32);
    let second_allocation: Extent in Granted & Vacant = second.taken;
    let tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = second.rest;
    let upper_and_tail: Extent in Granted & Vacant = partition.merge(Split {
        taken: second_allocation,
        rest: tail,
    });
    let restored: Extent in Granted & Vacant = partition.merge(Split {
        taken: first_allocation,
        rest: upper_and_tail,
    });
    restored as Extent
}

data Main {}

machine Main::main(&mut self) {}
